# No Silent Fallback

Code must never hide a failure or an unexpected state behind a silent success.

## Exception handling

Do not:

- rescue and ignore errors
- use `rescue nil`
- catch a broad exception only to continue as if nothing happened
- swallow JavaScript or TypeScript failures with empty `catch`, fallback return values, default
  responses, or best-effort continuation

Reason: swallowed exceptions hide failures and turn a hard error into silent data corruption.

## No "skip if missing" guards

Do not wrap an operation in a guard that silently no-ops when state is unexpected, for example a
`rename_table` wrapped in `return unless table_exists?(...)`. A silent skip hides a partial or failed
operation and lets state drift until it surfaces much later as a confusing failure.

When state may be inconsistent, fail loudly instead (see `fail-fast`).

## No masking default-value fallbacks

Do not substitute a default value to paper over a missing or invalid input when its absence means
something is actually wrong. A fallback is only acceptable when the absent value is a legitimate,
expected case — never as a way to avoid handling an error.

Do not hide invalid configuration by supplying defaults for required settings. Required
configuration must be explicit and must fail clearly when missing.

Read required Ruby environment variables with one-argument `ENV.fetch("NAME")`. Do not use
`ENV["NAME"]` for required configuration because a missing value returns `nil` and can continue
silently. Do not pass a default value or block to `ENV.fetch`; missing required configuration must
raise immediately.

Forbidden required-configuration patterns include:

- `ENV["NAME"]`, `ENV.fetch("NAME", "default")`, `ENV.fetch("NAME") { ... }`,
  `ENV["NAME"] || "default"`, or equivalent Ruby defaults
- `Rails.application.credentials.dig(...) || "default"` or equivalent credential defaults
- `process.env.NAME ?? "default"`, `process.env.NAME || "default"`,
  `import.meta.env.NAME ?? "default"`, or equivalent JavaScript or TypeScript defaults

Validate required environment variables at startup or module boundaries and raise or throw a clear
error when they are missing.
